 <exercises xml:id="hw-poisson-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



<exercise label="ex-poisson-er-triage">
    <title>Emergency Room Patient Intake</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            During a busy weekend shift, trauma patients arrive at an urban emergency room triage desk according to a Poisson process with a mean rate of 80 patients per hour. If the triage nurse steps away to grab a clean stethoscope, leaving the desk unattended for exactly one minute, what is the probability that at least 1 patient arrives during that brief interval?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.2636</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that <em>zero</em> patients arrive during the one-minute window (<m>P(Y=0)</m>). Remember to use the complement rule to find the probability of <m>\text{at least } 1</m> arrival.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p><m>0.7364</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! 
                    First, convert the hourly arrival rate to a 1-minute time block: <m>\lambda = \frac{80 \text{ patients}}{60 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{4}{3} \approx 1.3333</m> arrivals per minute.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then, apply the complement rule: 
                    <me>P(Y \ge 1) = 1 - P(Y = 0) = 1 - \frac{(1.3333)^0 e^{-1.3333}}{0!} = 1 - e^{-1.3333} \approx 1 - 0.2636 = 0.7364</me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.3515</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that <em>exactly</em> one patient arrives (<m>P(Y=1)</m>). The question requires the probability of <m>\text{at least } 1</m>, which accounts for multiple simultaneous patient walk-ins.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.5488</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. It looks like you didn't properly scale your time interval or miscalculated the exponent. Double-check your calculation for converting 80 patients per hour down to a single minute.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-poisson-campus-security">
    <title>Campus Security Patrol Allocation</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose that a campus security department devises a randomized patrol pattern to ensure unpredictability. A security vehicle may visit a high-priority quad location <m>Y = 0, 1, 2, 3, \dots</m> times per one-hour night shift block. Over a long observation window, records show each location is visited an average of once per hour. Assume that <m>Y</m> follows a Poisson probability distribution.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Identification Criteria & Lambda Identification -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-id">
        <statement>
            <p>
                How could you identify that <m>Y</m> is a Poisson random variable if that information had not been explicitly provided? 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts random, independent events occurring over a fixed continuum of time, where the rate is proportional to the interval size. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! The Poisson distribution models the counts of independent occurrences over a continuous interval (like an hour) given a known average rate (<m>\lambda = 1</m> visit per hour).</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts independent trials that can only result in binary success or failure outcomes from a fixed set of trials. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description fits a Binomial distribution. Poisson distributions do not have a fixed upper limit of discrete trials.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable tracks the total number of consecutive trials required until the very first success is observed. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description defines a Geometric random variable. Here, we are counting occurrences within a standard time block.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The visits have a uniform chance of happening at any point, meaning the count is steady. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Uniform distributions track spatial or temporal position variations, not accumulated integer incidence tallies. Also, \(\lambda\) is the mean rate, which is 1.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
<choice>
                <statement><p>It tracks changing success probabilities across consecutive non-replacement choices. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. That description applies to a Hypergeometric distribution, not a time-series event sequence.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task 2: Calculate Missing Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-miss">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the probability that the campus security officer will completely miss a given high-priority location during a specific one-hour time period. In other words, compute  <m>P(Y = 0)</m>. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
        <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.368"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the Poisson probability mass function <m>P(Y = y) = \frac{\lambda^y e^{-\lambda}}{y!}</m> with parameter <m>\lambda = 1</m>:
                <me>P(Y = 0) = \frac{1^0 \cdot e^{-1}}{0!} = \frac{1 \cdot e^{-1}}{1} = e^{-1} \approx 0.368</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Task 3: Calculate More Than Once Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-more">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that the location will be visited more than once during a single one-hour shift block?
            </p>
               <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.264"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!                 To find <m>P(Y \gt 1)</m>, we utilize the complement rule: 
                <me>P(Y \gt 1) = 1 - P(Y \le 1) = 1 - [P(Y = 0) + P(Y = 1)]</me>
                We already know <m>P(Y = 0) \approx 0.3679</m>. Next, calculate <m>P(Y = 1)</m>:
                <me>P(Y = 1) = \frac{1^1 \cdot e^{-1}}{1!} = e^{-1} \approx 0.3679</me>
                Combine and subtract from 1:
                <me>P(Y \gt 1) = 1 - (0.3679 + 0.3679) = 1 - 0.7358 = 0.2642</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the final probability value is <m>0.264</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise label="ex-poisson-panel-flaws" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Poisson Processes: Aerospace Structural Integrity Audits</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An aerospace manufacturing quality control audit shows that microscopic surface material flaws in high-performance carbon-fiber fuselage panels occur completely randomly and independently at a constant average rate of one flaw every 270 square feet. A technician pulls a newly manufactured rectangular section measuring exactly 9 feet by 13 feet for testing.
        </p>
        <p>
            Let <m>X</m> denote the total number of structural flaws contained within this specific panel segment. Because flaws are distributed randomly across a continuous spatial region, <m>X</m> follows a Poisson distribution. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Finding Lambda -->
    <task xml:id="task-panel-find-lambda">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the appropriate expected rate parameter <m>\lambda</m> (the mean number of flaws) for this specific 9-foot by 13-foot fuselage panel. 
             </p>
             <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.4333"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
             First, calculate the total surface area (<m>A</m>) of the inspected fuselage panel by multiplying its physical length and width dimensions:
                <me>A = 9 \text{ ft} \times 13 \text{ ft} = 117 \text{ sq ft}</me>
                Next, scale the baseline spatial material flaw density rate (<m>\alpha = \frac{1}{270}</m> flaws per square foot) by the target panel area to find the segment's expected value parameter <m>\lambda</m>:
                <me>\lambda = \alpha \cdot A = \frac{1 \text{ flaw}}{270 \text{ sq ft}} \cdot 117 \text{ sq ft} = \frac{117}{270} = \frac{13}{30} \approx 0.433333</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
       </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Compute Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-panel-compute-prob">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Using your  rate parameter from Part A, calculate the probability that this specific aerospace panel contains absolutely no flaws.
                 </p>
                       <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.6483"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
         To find the probability of observing exactly zero events in a continuous spatial Poisson process, evaluate the Poisson probability mass function (PMF) at <m>k = 0</m> using your unrounded rate parameter <m>\lambda = \frac{13}{30}</m>:
                <me>P(X = k) = \frac{\lambda^k \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{k!}</me>
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\left(\frac{13}{30}\right)^0 \cdot e^{-\frac{13}{30}}}{0!} = \frac{1 \cdot e^{-0.433333}}{1} \approx 0.648344</me> Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that the high-performance structural panel contains no surface flaws is approximately <strong>0.6483</strong> (or a 64.83% chance).
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
       </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      
    </task>
</exercise>
   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-poisson-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>

  


    <exercise label="ex-static-bitcoin-poisson">
        <title>Analyzing Cryptographic Hashing Probabilities</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
        Bitcoin mining utilizes an algorithmic process called cryptographic hashing, which structurally behaves like a sequence of millions of independent Bernoulli trials. Each time a computing node performs a single hash, it has a tiny probability of <m>p = 1.15 \times 10^{-23}</m> of successfully solving the current network difficulty puzzle. Solving a block rewards the miner with a subsidy of <m>3.125</m> Bitcoins. 
    </p>
    <p>
        These operations are executed by specialized Application-Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) computers called miners. A typical enterprise miner performs approximately <m>9.3 \times 10^{13}</m> hashes per second. Suppose a commercial operation deploys a farm array of <m>10</m> identical miners and runs them continuously for exactly one year.
    </p>
    </introduction>
        <!-- Part A: Total Trials Calculation -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-total-hashes">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Determine the total cumulative number of hashes performed by the 10-miner farm array over the course of a full 365-day year.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the total number of independent trials (<m>n</m>), we scale the hashrate per second by the number of seconds in a standard year across the full equipment cluster:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>n \amp = (9.3 \times 10^{13} \text{ hashes/sec}) \cdot 60 \text{ sec/min} \cdot 60 \text{ min/hr} \cdot 24 \text{ hr/day} \cdot 365 \text{ days/yr} \cdot 10 \text{ miners}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 9.3 \times 10^{13} \cdot 31,536,000 \cdot 10</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 2.933 \times 10^{22} \text{ total hashes}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The total number of trials executed by the hardware cluster in one year is <m>2.933 \times 10^{22}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Binomial Distribution Setup -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-binomial-parameters">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>X</m> represent the exact number of blocks successfully mined by the farm array during the year. Explain why <m>X</m> can be modeled as a Binomial random variable, and define its structural distribution parameters.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The random variable <m>X</m> satisfies all core conditions of a Binomial distribution: there is a fixed number of trials (<m>n</m>), each individual hash has a binary outcome (either the block solution is valid or invalid), the trials are physically independent based on the SHA-256 network protocol design, and the target success probability (<m>p</m>) remains constant across every roll.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The appropriate modeling parameters derived from our baseline system are:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Total Trials: <m>n = 2.933 \times 10^{22}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Success Probability: <m>p = 1.15 \times 10^{-23}</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Poisson Approximation Parameter -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-poisson-lambda">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the Binomial model defined in Part B can be well approximated by a Poisson distribution, and calculate the appropriate value for the rate parameter <m>\lambda</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Evaluating exact Binomial formulas using an enormous trial footprint (<m>n \approx 2.9 \times 10^{22}</m>) paired with an extremely small success weight (<m>p \approx 1.1 \times 10^{-23}</m>) causes direct computer arithmetic overflow failures. Because <m>n</m> is infinitely large and <m>p</m> is vanishingly close to zero, the distribution shape converges perfectly to a stable **Poisson approximation** using the expected rate scale parameter <m>\lambda = n \cdot p</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\lambda \amp = n \cdot p</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = (2.933 \times 10^{22}) \cdot (1.15 \times 10^{-23})</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0.3373 \text{ expected blocks per year}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The corresponding Poisson rate parameter is exactly <m>\lambda = 0.3373</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part D: Complement Probability Analysis -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-prob-at-least-one">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Using your Poisson approximation model, determine the mathematical probability that the hardware cluster successfully mines at least one block over the course of the year (<m>P(X \ge 1)</m>).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To compute <m>P(X \ge 1)</m> efficiently, we apply the standard **Complement Rule**: <m>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</m>. First, isolate the baseline probability of mining absolutely zero blocks using our Poisson PMF:
                    <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\lambda^0 \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{0!} = \frac{1 \cdot e^{-0.3373}}{1} \approx 0.7137</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Subtracting this empty outcome space from total certainty:
                    <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0) = 1 - 0.7137 = 0.2863</me>
                    The probability that the farm array secures at least one block reward over the year is approximately <strong>0.2863</strong> (or 28.63%).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part E: Bounded Interval Evaluation -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-prob-interval">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the probability that the mining operation secures between 2 and 4 blocks inclusive over the annual tracking window. In other words, compute <m>P(2 \le X \le 4)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Because the Poisson distribution models distinct, discrete events, the probability of falling within a closed integer interval is found by summing the point weights of the isolated components:
                    <me>P(2 \le X \le 4) = P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) + P(X = 4)</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Expanding each coordinate element using the Poisson PMF formula where <m>\lambda = 0.3373</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(2 \le X \le 4) \amp = \frac{\lambda^2 \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{2!} + \frac{\lambda^3 \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{3!} + \frac{\lambda^4 \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{4!}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{(0.3373)^2 \cdot e^{-0.3373}}{2} + \frac{(0.3373)^3 \cdot e^{-0.3373}}{6} + \frac{(0.3373)^4 \cdot e^{-0.3373}}{24}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp \approx 0.04065 + 0.00457 + 0.00039</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp \approx 0.04561</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Rounded to four decimal places, the probability of mining between 2 and 4 blocks is approximately <strong>0.0456</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>


   </exercises>

  </section>
